WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.040
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I want to talk about how I study

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.380
Italian or how I learned Italian.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.430
Uh, remember if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.890
And if you've listen to podcasts, on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.480
Uh, I do appreciate it.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:26.040
Now, uh, I did a video on what I did
to learn German, how I studied German,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.595
how I learned German to the extent
that I speak German, because it's

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:31.064
not one of my strongest languages.

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:33.825
Now I'm going to do the same with Italian.

00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:39.525
And, um, I don't, I haven't scoured
all my bookshelves to find all the

00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:44.715
books that I, I used for Italian, but
the process was essentially the same.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:49.515
And now it is again, you
know, I had traveled in Italy.

00:00:49.545 --> 00:00:55.140
I had hitchhiked, uh, hitchhiked
in Italy, uh, with the knowledge of

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.090
Spanish that I had, I was kind of
communicating with people in Italy.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.160
Uh, and it's always a language that
had, you know, a great appeal to me

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.080
because Italy is such a lovely country.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.940
There's so many lovely places to visit
and food and music and lively people.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.640
And so I wanted to be good in Italian.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.250
And of course I wasn't,
I was communicate...

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.410
communicating, but at a very basic level.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.964
So, I can't remember when I decided
to make a greater effort with

00:01:25.964 --> 00:01:30.074
my Italian, but I will show you
some of the things that I did.

00:01:30.554 --> 00:01:37.965
So, first of all, the one thing I
remember is that I got a hold of

00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:42.804
the Linguaphone series for Italian.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:47.585
And Linguaphone is, I don't know if
they still exist, I guess they do.

00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:52.125
It's extremely expensive for what it
is, because essentially what you need

00:01:52.125 --> 00:01:56.385
is text with audio and a glossary.

00:01:56.535 --> 00:01:57.225
That's all you need.

00:01:57.255 --> 00:01:58.995
You don't need all the exercises.

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:04.975
It's fashionable now to have
pre-reading questions and post-reading

00:02:05.025 --> 00:02:09.435
comprehension questions, and all kinds
of things that are quite unnecessary.

00:02:09.465 --> 00:02:12.725
In fact, that spoil the
pleasure of reading.

00:02:13.295 --> 00:02:17.970
Uh, so there's a lot of useless
exercise stuff in here, but I had

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.350
to find something for Italian.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.850
So I don't know if I got a deal on it
or if it was sort of surplus somewhere.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:28.860
But I do know that I went through this and
listened to the audio many, many times.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.660
And this was before I got into this, you
know, the realization that you have to

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.100
listen repetitively over and over again,
instinctively that's something I did.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.850
And I can remember listening when
the family arrived in Milan and

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.560
vaguely, this would be at least
30 years ago that I did this.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.600
So I went through this.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:57.690
Now, um, then with my experience in
German of finding, you know, readers with

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:03.980
glossaries, um, you know, I found books
like this First Itlaian Reader for Adults.

00:03:05.144 --> 00:03:08.685
And I have never liked, I do have,
I don't know where they are here.

00:03:08.685 --> 00:03:13.454
I can find them, you know, sort of
bilingual books where you have, let's

00:03:13.454 --> 00:03:16.665
say the Italian on the left-hand side
and the English on the right-hand side.

00:03:16.845 --> 00:03:21.945
I've never found that very effective
because what I need is the words, the

00:03:21.945 --> 00:03:25.905
individual words that I don't understand,
I don't want to have to read through

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:30.045
the English to find the spot that
corresponds to where I am in the Italian.

00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:34.065
What I want is like, here's this
word I don't know what it means.

00:03:34.305 --> 00:03:35.145
What does it mean?

00:03:35.925 --> 00:03:41.685
And in former days that meant, uh,
you know, readers with glossaries.

00:03:41.985 --> 00:03:48.015
As I showed you for German, the
disadvantage is that very often, the

00:03:48.015 --> 00:03:52.485
word you need is not in their glossary
or the word that they introduced in the

00:03:52.485 --> 00:03:54.855
glossary is a word you already know.

00:03:55.275 --> 00:04:01.665
So to a large extent that has now been
made obsolete by online dictionaries,

00:04:01.665 --> 00:04:03.345
by LingQ and things of that nature.

00:04:03.795 --> 00:04:07.755
But I did a lot of reading
of, and I just couldn't find

00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:11.035
them, but oh, here we have...

00:04:11.155 --> 00:04:12.255
here's another one.

00:04:12.525 --> 00:04:15.015
But there were lots of more
and, and, uh, always, you

00:04:15.015 --> 00:04:17.235
know, readings with glossaries.

00:04:17.265 --> 00:04:21.795
Now, obviously, if you know French,
if you know Spanish, a lot of

00:04:21.795 --> 00:04:24.665
vocabulary is freebie in Italian.

00:04:25.409 --> 00:04:28.530
People always make a big
deal about false friends.

00:04:29.039 --> 00:04:32.820
To me, there is the odd false
friend in these languages.

00:04:32.849 --> 00:04:34.229
It's not a big deal.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:41.250
The common vocabulary is a much greater
benefit in learning a language and

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.469
the odd word that is a false friend.

00:04:43.479 --> 00:04:46.380
You quickly find out if you
use it in the wrong context.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.380
And so you make adjustments, but it's...

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.739
false friends, in other words,
a common vocabulary is a

00:04:52.739 --> 00:04:53.799
good thing not a bad thing.

00:04:54.405 --> 00:04:56.835
Um, but with the Italian...

00:04:57.015 --> 00:05:01.815
now grammar, of course, this is a
great little grammar book, which you

00:05:01.815 --> 00:05:05.294
go back, I go back to over and over
and over again, because all the little

00:05:05.294 --> 00:05:13.755
pronouns ... and that, uh, are difficult
because they're different, different from

00:05:13.755 --> 00:05:15.495
what I am used to in other languages.

00:05:15.495 --> 00:05:20.055
And so kind of reviewed the rules and then
you watch for them when you're reading

00:05:20.055 --> 00:05:21.615
and then you listen and stuff like that.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:30.355
A big, a big boost to my Italian was
the discovery of il narratore, which is

00:05:30.375 --> 00:05:39.585
a, an audio book publisher, very small
based near, um, I think he's near, uh,

00:05:40.224 --> 00:05:43.515
Verona, uh, but he's up on the hillside.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.500
And he's there and he puts out wonderful,
uh, audio books and he does a wonderful

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.280
rendering of  I promessi sposi.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:56.599
Now, a lot of people, especially
Italian people, they had,  I promessi

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:00.060
sposi, which is a classic of Italian
literature, they had it at school.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:00.900
They don't like it.

00:06:01.049 --> 00:06:04.200
It's boring, but for me, it's what I like.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:05.280
It's exotic.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.539
It's another century.

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:07.979
It's another country.

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:14.260
And of course, with, uh, the wonderful,
uh, audio book version that...

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.590
mauricio does, it's lovely.

00:06:17.159 --> 00:06:21.870
And I can remember jogging when
I was in Palm Springs, listening

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.825
to I promessi sposi in Italian.

00:06:25.065 --> 00:06:29.055
He also does a wonderful version
of wonderful version of Pinocchio.

00:06:29.325 --> 00:06:33.935
And the advantage with both those texts
is that I can import the text to LingQ.

00:06:34.275 --> 00:06:35.715
I can look at words and phrases.

00:06:35.775 --> 00:06:39.795
So I'm no longer a, you know, in many
cases, you know, if you get a book

00:06:40.515 --> 00:06:46.065
like a modern book like this, Alberto
Moravia, uh, there won't be, uh, you

00:06:46.065 --> 00:06:49.995
know, you can't just get the digital
text to import to LingQ, but if you're

00:06:49.995 --> 00:06:54.405
dealing with, um, classics, you can
import the text, look up words and

00:06:54.405 --> 00:06:56.205
phrases, you can listen to the audio.

00:06:56.474 --> 00:07:00.075
And so you're learning in a way
that's agreeable, it's easy.

00:07:00.075 --> 00:07:05.205
That's much easier than struggling
with bilingual textbooks or even

00:07:05.325 --> 00:07:06.885
books with, with glossaries.

00:07:07.275 --> 00:07:11.534
So that was my path with Italian.

00:07:12.044 --> 00:07:15.104
And, but I'm not where
I want to be in Italian.

00:07:15.375 --> 00:07:22.245
For example, if I want to read
Umberto Eco, which I do, there are too

00:07:22.245 --> 00:07:24.315
many words that I don't understand.

00:07:24.975 --> 00:07:27.945
And so if I start reading them and if
I look through this book, I'll see a

00:07:27.945 --> 00:07:31.845
lot of underlinings where, you know,
I underlined the word with the sort of

00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:35.805
optimistic intention of eventually looking
it up at a dictionary, which I never do.

00:07:36.345 --> 00:07:42.285
Um, ideally I would be able to read those
on LingQ so I can look up every word and

00:07:42.285 --> 00:07:44.265
save words and phrases and learn them.

00:07:44.625 --> 00:07:50.190
So with that, I'll just show you a
very brief, uh, what I have done with

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:55.680
Italian on LingQ, and then I'll kind
of, uh, finish up with a few words.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.040
Okay.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.630
Let's have a look at what I've
been doing in LingQ, which has

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.640
really made the learning of a...

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.740
of any language, including Italian a lot
easier than it was 30 years ago when I

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.730
decided to improve my level of Italian.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:20.335
So if I click on my thumbnail here and,
um, No, I haven't done anything recently,

00:08:20.335 --> 00:08:32.065
but if I go to my all time will see that
I have 22,500 or 22,236, known words that

00:08:32.065 --> 00:08:38.845
I was particularly active terms of adding
to my known words total back in 2008

00:08:39.804 --> 00:08:42.085
and have been active at various times.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:44.910
Uh, this is the known words.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:49.770
Now, if I look at my LingQs created
number there's only 5,000 of them,

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:55.560
so that the bulk of the words that
are on my known words total are words

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.640
that I saw in the text, but didn't
need to look up cause I knew them.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:05.099
And so I think eventually sort of
determines what your vocabulary

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.930
level is as you will go by saving
words that you need to learn.

00:09:10.635 --> 00:09:15.225
Uh, if I look at words of reading that
I've done on LingQ, I hadn't read a

00:09:15.225 --> 00:09:19.435
fair amount, but I've also read a lot
of Italian away from LingQ so LingQ

00:09:19.435 --> 00:09:27.585
hasn't been my main, um, place to study
Italian, although, you know, 61,000

00:09:27.585 --> 00:09:33.495
words back in the summer, I guess
the end of 2013 is not insignificant.

00:09:34.095 --> 00:09:37.875
Uh, but, uh, that gives you, so I
found out that the gradual growth

00:09:37.875 --> 00:09:41.610
in my known words, And picked up
a lot at the beginning and have

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:45.420
continued to increase gradually, but
I really haven't been very active.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:50.670
So that's really the picture
of my activity, uh, when it

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.760
comes to Italian on LingQ.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.250
So, um, you know, where do I want
to get to with, with Itlaian?

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:02.850
You know, I can communicate in Italian,
uh, to some extent, but not as well as I

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:06.540
would, like, I don't speak it as well as
I would like, I understand quite well.

00:10:07.365 --> 00:10:15.285
Uh, but if I pick up a book like this,
like, uh, I have actually, uh, I think, I

00:10:15.285 --> 00:10:18.415
think this is one where I found the audio
book, so I could listen to the audiobook

00:10:18.435 --> 00:10:24.255
of what if I read through it on every page
I can see because I've underlined them.

00:10:24.645 --> 00:10:29.355
I can see five or six or
so words that I don't know.

00:10:29.505 --> 00:10:30.705
So I'm not good at it.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.720
In my reading, I would like to be better.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:39.090
And if I weren't learning Arabic
and Persian, then just as with all

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:44.370
the other languages, I would love to
spend time to get better in Italian.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:49.410
But I have sort of brought my
Italian up to a level where it's

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.290
not just sort of semi Spanish.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.370
It's actually Italian but limited.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.710
And, but I am at a situation where if
I were to go to Italy or if I've had

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.890
the opportunity or the need to use the
language, or if I just decided to spend

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:10.230
a month indulging in my interest and
the enjoyment that I get from Italian,

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.810
that I could, you know, bring it up.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:15.420
And so it's kind of on
the same level as German.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.680
In other words, a rusty B2
that I could easily bring up

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.960
to a, a better level of B2.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.300
And that's where I hope
to get my language.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:29.760
As I continue to explore new languages, I
will leave you with a couple of interviews

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:34.740
that I've done in Italian so that you
can gauge, you know, the difficulties

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.600
that I continue to have in Italian.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:37.689
Thank you for listening.

